00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:15.322
my Twitter information is up
there, also, my GitHub handle.

00:01:15.322 --> 00:01:17.280
All of the demos that
we're going to see today,

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.600
and actually, a lot
of the technology

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.730
that we're going to see
today, it's all open source.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:21.810
And this is all part
of one of the things

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:24.850
that we're trying to do, which
is get more and more openness,

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:26.490
more into the open
source community.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.948
And everything you're going to
see here, large parts of it,

00:01:28.948 --> 00:01:30.900
all open source.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.150
So before we degrade in,
just a little bit-- before we

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.691
go build demos on App Engine
and learn a bunch of cool stuff,

00:01:35.691 --> 00:01:37.650
what is App Engine?

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:39.840
At the end of the day,
it's a very simple way

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.210
to take your code,
give it to us,

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:45.960
and run it on top of Google's
infrastructure at scale.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.690
So really, the fundamental thing
that we want you to walk away

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.600
with is, you bring your code,
and we handle running it

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.682
on Google's infrastructure.

00:01:53.682 --> 00:01:56.140
And when you're running it on
Google's infrastructure, what

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:57.360
you're really doing
is running it inside

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:58.985
of a data center
that's been proven out

00:01:58.985 --> 00:02:01.590
for almost the last 20 years,
the same technology that's

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:05.760
been built for Google
Search, Maps, Gmail.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.530
All these things that
we rely on day in

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.630
and day out, we have proven
infrastructure that's

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:11.921
gotten the job done for us
at Google and proven methods

00:02:11.921 --> 00:02:12.880
for getting it done.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.380
And we think we
want to make it easy

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:17.030
for you to do the same
thing with App Engine.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:19.534
So what would you build
on top of App Engine?

00:02:19.534 --> 00:02:21.200
Well, there are a
couple of sweet spots.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.180
And we've seen that several
Compute offerings today,

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:24.930
and we're going to get
into those in a little bit.

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:26.720
But really, the sweet
spot for App Engine

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.180
is web applications and APIs.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:31.450
So if you're building
a user-facing website,

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:33.979
be it in Node.js, Ruby, PHP--

00:02:33.979 --> 00:02:36.020
you know, any of your
traditional web languages--

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:37.220
it's really a great destination.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:38.928
Because you can just
build it, deploy it,

00:02:38.928 --> 00:02:41.750
and run it without having to
think about the ops or scale.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:43.700
It's also become
really popular back end

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:44.816
for mobile applications.

00:02:44.816 --> 00:02:46.940
There are some pretty
canonical examples out there,

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:49.940
folks like Snapchat, other
gaming companies that

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:51.230
are built on top of us.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:53.810
It's really easy to scale
up very, very quickly

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:54.504
on App Engine.

00:02:54.504 --> 00:02:56.420
And it's easy to get up
and running very fast.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:59.330
So it's been a popular
choice for mobile back ends.

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:03.230
With that really comes
just, also, HTTP APIs.

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:06.290
Even if it's not a traditional
mobile back end, a lot of us,

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:08.870
our applications
are powered by APIs.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:12.180
And App Engine integrates really
nicely with Cloud Endpoints,

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.070
another product
that we announced

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.760
a v2 of recently, which
makes it easy to do things

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:21.050
like metering, billing, logging,
authentication, all sitting

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:22.340
on top of your API.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:23.840
And Cloud Functions
and App Engine

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:25.460
make a really nice
fit together to go

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:27.020
build those kinds of things.

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:28.760
And then lastly,
it's a great fit

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:30.410
for line of business
applications.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:32.480
We're working with a
lot of enterprises today

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:34.040
that have a ton
of internal sites

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:37.185
that they need to build
quickly, maintain, and do it

00:03:37.185 --> 00:03:38.810
with a small staff
that they don't want

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:41.900
to have to build up expertise
in five different deployment

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:42.666
systems.

00:03:42.666 --> 00:03:44.540
And App Engine has been
a great fit for that.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:46.370
As a matter of fact,
it's running the majority

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:47.990
of a lot of our
internal apps that we

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:49.910
have at Google,
the user-facing web

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:52.520
applications we use for a
lot of our payroll systems,

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:54.890
for our learning and
management systems.

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:57.734
All of this stuff at Google
is actually increasingly built

00:03:57.734 --> 00:03:59.150
on top of App
Engine, and we think

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:02.590
it's great for other
companies to do that as well.

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:05.290
So where does App Engine
fit into the broader context

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:05.790
of Compute?

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:07.230
You know, we've heard
a lot of announcements.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:09.204
We saw the Cloud
Functions announcement.

00:04:09.204 --> 00:04:11.370
We have committed use
discounts on virtual machines.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:13.286
We've heard containers,
containers, containers

00:04:13.286 --> 00:04:15.060
and Kubernetes over
and over again.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:16.980
Where does App
Engine fit into this?

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:19.300
And when is it a
good choice to use?

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.180
So we'll start at the
bottom of the pyramid.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:22.680
When we think about
virtual machines

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:24.450
and we think about
Compute Engine, really,

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:26.130
what you're running
is infrastructure.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:27.640
So if you're an organization
that's picking up,

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:30.015
and you're moving something,
a bunch of applications that

00:04:30.015 --> 00:04:31.920
were built on traditional
virtual machines,

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:34.336
and you want to pick that up
and find a path to the cloud,

00:04:34.336 --> 00:04:35.970
usually, this is the
easiest first step

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:37.810
that you can take to
get into the cloud.

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:39.560
If you want to think
about infrastructure,

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:42.143
you want to think about VMs, you
want to think about networks,

00:04:42.143 --> 00:04:43.950
then this is a good fit.

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:47.417
One level up in that level of--
in that level of abstraction,

00:04:47.417 --> 00:04:48.750
you're going to have containers.

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:51.090
If you're an organization that's
already familiar with Docker

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:53.089
and you want to run a
fleet of Docker containers

00:04:53.089 --> 00:04:55.070
efficiently across
a fleet of machines,

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:57.919
Kubernetes is a
fantastic option.

00:04:57.919 --> 00:04:59.960
Now, the next step up when
you get to App Engine,

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:02.420
this is where we start thinking
less about infrastructure,

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:03.950
and we start
specifically thinking

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:06.080
about serverless-style
architectures.

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:08.720
You want to hand us our code
and ask Google to figure out

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:10.460
the most efficient
and cost effective

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:12.364
way to go run that code for you.

00:05:12.364 --> 00:05:14.030
And really, that's
what App Engine does.

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:16.250
Again, web
applications and APIs--

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:18.290
you give us the code, we run it.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:20.000
And then at the top,
if you have a system

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.580
where you want to respond
to very specific events--

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:25.147
think WebHooks-- or every time
a file is written into a GCS

00:05:25.147 --> 00:05:27.230
bucket, you want to have
a little bit of code that

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:29.960
reacts to that, that's where
Cloud Functions becomes

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:30.680
a perfect bet.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:33.170
Instead of thinking at the
application or service level,

00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:35.990
you're now thinking at the
function and event abstraction.

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:37.700
So this is just kind of the
examples of the various levels

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:39.200
of Compute we have
and where I think

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.139
your applications can fit in.

00:05:41.139 --> 00:05:42.680
So with that-- you
know, it was a lot

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:43.760
of talking-- let's get into it.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.370
This one is seven
or eight tricks.

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:47.360
I'm going to be honest,
until last night,

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:48.985
I didn't have the
eighth trick working.

00:05:48.985 --> 00:05:51.140
So I wasn't sure if I
was going to have eight.

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:54.020
But we'll see, at the
end, if it worked out.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:56.990
So trick number one, you
can go from your code

00:05:56.990 --> 00:06:00.330
to production in
a single command.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:03.330
That command is
gcloud app deploy.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:05.032
Now, this is part
of the Cloud SDK.

00:06:05.032 --> 00:06:07.240
And this might be something
that we take for granted,

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:08.864
especially if you've
been an App Engine

00:06:08.864 --> 00:06:10.736
user for a long time
it's been out there.

00:06:10.736 --> 00:06:13.110
But when you think about what
I would normally go through

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:14.550
with a deployment,
I could deploy

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:15.925
to an individual
virtual machine.

00:06:15.925 --> 00:06:18.210
I could take these files
and upload the one VM.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.310
But what happens when my
application gets popular

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:21.575
and it needs to scale up?

00:06:21.575 --> 00:06:22.950
What happens when,
maybe, there's

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:25.409
trouble in the zone where I've
deployed my virtual machine?

00:06:25.409 --> 00:06:27.325
What happens when, you
know, something changes

00:06:27.325 --> 00:06:29.670
with the-- there's a problem
with the underlying disk?

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:32.340
A lot of things can go wrong
when we run on one instance.

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:33.750
And if we're just deploying
to a single machine,

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:36.090
we're putting our application,
and potentially, our business,

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:36.730
at risk.

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:39.190
And we need to think about
a lot of these problems.

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:41.130
So when you create a
deployment in App Engine,

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:42.270
we're going to do
a couple of things.

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:43.520
We're going to take your code.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:46.149
We're going to upload it
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:06:46.149 --> 00:06:47.190
We're going to take that.

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:49.590
And you don't need to
understand anything about Docker

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:50.520
to use App Engine.

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:52.800
But under the hood, what
we're actually doing is

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:55.080
we're building your code
along with our runtimes

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.522
that we build into a container.

00:06:57.522 --> 00:06:58.980
And then that
container gets pushed

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:00.539
to Google Container Registry.

00:07:00.539 --> 00:07:02.580
And now it's available
for all of your App Engine

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:04.090
applications.

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:06.747
We set up a Google Cloud load
balancer, which, this is new.

00:07:06.747 --> 00:07:08.580
We're now using the
same Cloud load balancer

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:10.200
across all of Google's
infrastructure

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:12.424
is coming in the same path.

00:07:12.424 --> 00:07:14.340
And then we're going to
take your application,

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:17.080
and we're going to run it in
three availabilities zones.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:18.550
Now, the reason
that we do this is

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:21.962
that there may be trouble in an
individual zone at any point.

00:07:21.962 --> 00:07:23.920
You know, generally, this
stuff is very stable.

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:26.372
But sometimes things in
individual zones happen.

00:07:26.372 --> 00:07:28.330
So what we do is
automatically, out of the box,

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:30.730
configure App Engine to deploy
to three different availability

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:32.630
zones so that even if
we do have a problem,

00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:34.380
your application is
going to automatically

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:36.670
rebalance and keep
serving to your customers.

00:07:36.670 --> 00:07:38.320
And then within
each of those zones,

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:40.485
they all independently
autoscale.

00:07:40.485 --> 00:07:41.860
So as the use of
your application

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:44.740
grows, and as traffic increases,
and as that use contracts

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:47.110
again, we're going to
automatically provision

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:50.152
and release new instances
to serve your application.

00:07:53.319 --> 00:07:55.110
So along the way-- so
we just talked mainly

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:57.455
about application instances
and the autoscalers--

00:07:57.455 --> 00:07:58.830
there are a bunch
of other things

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:00.450
that we're setting up
out of the box, stuff

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:02.610
that, when you deploy an
app to that individual VM,

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:04.710
you have to think about
at one point or another.

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:06.137
You have to think about logging.

00:08:06.137 --> 00:08:07.470
How are you capturing your logs?

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:08.510
Where are you
sending those logs?

00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:10.110
Is that log system
going to scale?

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:12.360
Can you query through it
efficiently when you need to?

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:13.950
When you think
about autoscaling,

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:15.300
what do you happen
when you get popular?

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:16.020
Traffic goes up.

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:17.480
Traffic comes back down.

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:20.370
Load balancing, monitoring,
health checking,

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:22.877
configuring SSL, these
are all additional things

00:08:22.877 --> 00:08:24.960
that, when you go to set
up a site or a deployment

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:26.460
on your own, you
have to internalize

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:28.440
and you have to think
about instead of building

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:30.460
your application or
focusing on your business.

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:33.600
And they're all things that
we aim to do with App Engine.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:35.460
So one of the new
things that we have--

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:36.929
that we've had now for
a couple-- actually,

00:08:36.929 --> 00:08:39.000
for a couple of months we've
been working on-- or a couple

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:39.809
of years--

00:08:39.809 --> 00:08:41.700
is the App Engine
flexible environment.

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:45.390
So originally, App Engine was
available in Go or Python,

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:46.860
Java, and PHP.

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:49.874
And we've now expanded that
to include Node.js, Ruby.

00:08:49.874 --> 00:08:51.790
And the big announcement
yesterday, of course,

00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:54.781
is .NET going beta on
App Engine as well.

00:08:54.781 --> 00:08:56.530
And you can even bring
your own container.

00:08:56.530 --> 00:08:58.780
And this is something that
we're going to get a chance

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:00.370
to look at in a little bit.

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:02.040
One of the big changes we were
able to announce yesterday

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:04.380
is all these services that
you can run your own Docker

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:06.240
container, Node,
Ruby, you can do

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:09.736
all of this with a 99.95% SLA.

00:09:09.736 --> 00:09:11.610
And that's something
that we're able to offer

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:13.450
because, as of
yesterday, the App Engine

00:09:13.450 --> 00:09:14.960
flexible environment is GA.

00:09:17.770 --> 00:09:18.800
It's OK, you can clap.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:19.750
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:20.490
I need this.

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:20.990
Thank you.

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:26.680
So the first trick
was gcloud app deploy,

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:29.590
getting 99.95% SLA
with a single command

00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:31.010
and not having to
think about it.

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:32.950
Let's dig in a little
bit to the second trick.

00:09:32.950 --> 00:09:35.260
And this is one of
my favorite ones.

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:36.850
With App Engine, I
can split traffic

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:39.830
between multiple versions
of my application.

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:41.650
So why is this important?

00:09:41.650 --> 00:09:43.580
Think about that
deployment we just created.

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:44.657
So we pushed our code.

00:09:44.657 --> 00:09:45.490
We got it out there.

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:47.410
We have our multi-zonal release.

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:48.590
We've got many instances.

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:49.490
It's going great.

00:09:49.490 --> 00:09:51.430
But now I'm ready to
push a new version.

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:54.280
Well, how do I know that my
new version doesn't have a bug?

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:56.230
How do I know that it isn't
going to bring my app crashing

00:09:56.230 --> 00:09:56.980
down when I do it?

00:09:56.980 --> 00:09:59.410
What I want to do is I want
to deploy a second version

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:00.640
of my application.

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:03.650
I want to verify it
out-of-band, both with tests,

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:05.380
and I want to go
do manual checks.

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:07.260
And then slowly,
over time, I want

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:09.070
to take just a
little bit of traffic

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:11.440
and start sending it to
that new set of instances.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:12.700
I want to make sure
that it can scale up

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:13.420
the way I expect it to.

00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:15.070
I want to make sure that
things don't come crashing down

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:16.510
when I want to monitor errors.

00:10:16.510 --> 00:10:18.130
And then only when
I'm ready, and only

00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:20.540
when I'm sure that this
new deployment is good,

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:22.900
I want to transition
100% of my traffic

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:24.400
over to the new
version, and then

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:26.640
eventually, turn
down the old one.

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:29.124
And what this is called
is a no downtime upgrade.

00:10:29.124 --> 00:10:30.790
And it's something
that's very difficult

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:32.790
to get right on your own,
but one of the things,

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:34.540
with App Engine, we
make very simple.

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:36.250
Use gcloud app deploy.

00:10:36.250 --> 00:10:40.330
You set a traffic split however
you'd like to set it, verify,

00:10:40.330 --> 00:10:41.650
and then go to 100%.

00:10:41.650 --> 00:10:43.439
And it's really easy to set up.

00:10:43.439 --> 00:10:44.980
So another reason
that you might want

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:47.530
to go off and use our
traffic splitting feature

00:10:47.530 --> 00:10:50.440
is if you want to do
something like AB testing.

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:53.290
So let's imagine that I have
an application out there.

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:56.110
I'm an online grocer, and
I'm selling fruit to people.

00:10:56.110 --> 00:10:59.080
And I'm trying to decide,
which background image

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:01.600
is going to cause my
customers to come into my site

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:02.650
and buy more fruit.

00:11:02.650 --> 00:11:04.608
You know, what do they
think is more delicious?

00:11:04.608 --> 00:11:07.630
What gets them salivating
and buying things on my site?

00:11:07.630 --> 00:11:08.290
Is it apples?

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:09.340
Is it cherries?

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:10.360
Is it lemons?

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:13.569
Or is it the deliciously
pungent durian, you know?

00:11:13.569 --> 00:11:14.110
I don't know.

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:16.660
I don't know what's going to
go ahead and get them hungry.

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:18.539
So let's actually--
it's demo time.

00:11:18.539 --> 00:11:20.830
Let's figure out how we can
actually go build something

00:11:20.830 --> 00:11:24.080
like this on top of App Engine.

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:26.080
So what I've got here is
I have a site that I've

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:26.996
created on App Engine.

00:11:26.996 --> 00:11:28.250
It's got a single service.

00:11:28.250 --> 00:11:31.180
This single service has
four different versions

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:32.950
of my application,
each ones that I've

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:35.001
deployed with the gcloud
app deploy command.

00:11:35.001 --> 00:11:37.500
And you see, I've got the cherry
version, the apple version,

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:38.620
durian, and lemon.

00:11:38.620 --> 00:11:41.017
So if I click on "cherry,"
every time I do that,

00:11:41.017 --> 00:11:42.100
I'm going to get a cherry.

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:42.820
Looks pretty good.

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:43.861
Makes me a little hungry.

00:11:43.861 --> 00:11:45.730
It is lunchtime.

00:11:45.730 --> 00:11:48.474
I can click on "apple," and
I'm going to get my apples.

00:11:48.474 --> 00:11:49.390
And you get the point.

00:11:49.390 --> 00:11:50.806
I can click on
these individually.

00:11:50.806 --> 00:11:52.630
And any one at a
time, I can verify,

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:55.420
is going to give me the
fruit that it's named after.

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:57.910
But right now, all of my
customers are getting cherry.

00:11:57.910 --> 00:11:58.930
So every time they
come to the site,

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:00.190
they're going to get cherry.

00:12:00.190 --> 00:12:03.696
How do I enable a traffic split
to test out my hypothesis?

00:12:03.696 --> 00:12:05.320
You know, I'm going
to go with the idea

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:07.720
that I think Apple
is a better fit.

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:10.150
I can select all the versions
of my running application

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:11.010
right now.

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:12.534
I can choose Split traffic.

00:12:12.534 --> 00:12:14.950
And what I'm going to do is
I'm going to go ahead and say,

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:17.470
apple's going to get 33%.

00:12:17.470 --> 00:12:19.540
Lemon is going to get 33%.

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:21.364
And Durian, we're going
to put that at 1%,

00:12:21.364 --> 00:12:22.530
because we're still testing.

00:12:22.530 --> 00:12:26.450
I'm still unsure if the durian
is a good choice or not.

00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:29.540
And I'm going to go
ahead and save this.

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:31.210
So while this is
saving, the next thing

00:12:31.210 --> 00:12:33.730
that we're going to do is we're
going to click on Services.

00:12:33.730 --> 00:12:36.146
And we're actually going to
take a look at how this works.

00:12:36.146 --> 00:12:38.680
So I have a single service
right now, one service,

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:40.410
many versions under it.

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:42.370
And I'm going to go ahead
and launch this page.

00:12:42.370 --> 00:12:44.980
And it looks like, OK, the
first time, I got lemon.

00:12:44.980 --> 00:12:48.070
The second time, I got
cherry, and then apple,

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:49.810
and so on and so forth.

00:12:49.810 --> 00:12:51.034
So what we can actually do--

00:12:51.034 --> 00:12:52.450
the way I've set
up this site is I

00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:53.620
want to be able to track this.

00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:54.580
You know, maybe
you don't believe

00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:57.340
that we're actually doing that
traffic split exactly right.

00:12:57.340 --> 00:12:58.870
So let's go here.

00:12:58.870 --> 00:13:01.960
And I'm going to go over
to a dashboard that's

00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:04.360
actually tracking all of
the hits on this page.

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:05.804
And I see somebody
hitting apple.

00:13:05.804 --> 00:13:08.220
I don't know if anyone was
able, actually, to see that URL

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:09.220
or not.

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:11.881
And so this is the point in the
talk where I want everybody--

00:13:11.881 --> 00:13:13.630
usually speakers don't
like it when you're

00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:14.800
on your phones or laptop.

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:16.840
I'm looking at you,
guy with the laptop.

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:19.267
It wasn't actually you.

00:13:19.267 --> 00:13:20.350
Go ahead and get them out.

00:13:20.350 --> 00:13:21.100
Get out your phone.

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:21.933
Get out your laptop.

00:13:21.933 --> 00:13:23.710
And I want everyone
to try this out.

00:13:23.710 --> 00:13:27.015
And we're going to watch it in
real time and see what happens.

00:13:27.015 --> 00:13:29.140
So I want everyone to go
to fruit.appengine.io--

00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:34.350
so fruit.appengine.io--
and let's see--

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:37.970
we're watching people hit these
pages, coming in real time.

00:13:37.970 --> 00:13:39.960
Looks like, right now,
apples are out to lead.

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:42.240
Cherries are catching up.

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:45.120
We're going to keep doing this
until that 1% person manages

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:48.648
to-- oh, who got the durian?

00:13:48.648 --> 00:13:50.550
Ah, come on, someone
has to admit to it.

00:13:50.550 --> 00:13:52.680
Somebody got the durian.

00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:53.650
It was you.

00:13:53.650 --> 00:13:54.150
Was it?

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:54.649
Come on up.

00:13:54.649 --> 00:13:55.365
Come up on stage.

00:13:59.822 --> 00:14:00.780
You are the big winner.

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:03.113
I can't believe it was you
that actually won the durian.

00:14:03.113 --> 00:14:04.560
That's amazing.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:06.975
What I have for you, sir,
is an actual durian--

00:14:06.975 --> 00:14:08.790
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:08.790 --> 00:14:11.730
--that I got in
Chinatown this morning.

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:16.410
So please do not cut that here.

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:17.910
Take that home.

00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:19.917
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] .

00:14:19.917 --> 00:14:22.500
JUSTIN BECKWITH: That was a lot
heavier than I expected it to,

00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:25.920
I'm not going to lie.

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:27.270
Yeah.

00:14:27.270 --> 00:14:28.930
Enterprise scenarios
here folks, that's

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:32.481
what we're focusing
on this week.

00:14:32.481 --> 00:14:33.980
All right, so that's
one of the ways

00:14:33.980 --> 00:14:35.570
that we can use
traffic splitting

00:14:35.570 --> 00:14:38.390
to both do safe upgrades
and to make sure

00:14:38.390 --> 00:14:42.540
that we have the best version
of our site for A/B testing.

00:14:42.540 --> 00:14:43.790
By the way, all those images--

00:14:43.790 --> 00:14:44.780
Creative Commons.

00:14:44.780 --> 00:14:46.780
Thank you to the very
nice people who made them.

00:14:46.780 --> 00:14:48.350
They were beautiful.

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:50.570
So that was trick number
two was traffic splitting.

00:14:50.570 --> 00:14:53.210
Let's take a look at
trick number three.

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:54.140
And this is a big one.

00:14:54.140 --> 00:14:58.430
As of this week, you can now run
.NET core applications on App

00:14:58.430 --> 00:14:59.780
Engine.

00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:02.540
And that's because ASP..NET,
on App Engine yesterday,

00:15:02.540 --> 00:15:04.830
went beta.

00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:07.560
So what that means is that
we have a .NET core runtime.

00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:10.500
We've got a full list
of tutorials, samples.

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:12.630
The same runtime you
can use on App Engine,

00:15:12.630 --> 00:15:15.240
you can actually pick up and
use it on Container Engine

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:18.240
and run it there as well
inside of a Linux VM.

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:20.730
All the client libraries-- so
the things that use to access

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:25.094
services like Spanner, the
Cloud Vision API, Datastore,

00:15:25.094 --> 00:15:27.510
all these services and APIs
that we have out there are now

00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:30.180
available via NuGet
packages on .NET.

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:32.279
And finally, some tooling--

00:15:32.279 --> 00:15:33.320
all of this is available.

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:36.420
You can learn about it on
cloud.google.com/dotnet.

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:38.960
And it's got all of that
getting started documentation.

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:41.494
The runtimes, the client
libraries, the docs,

00:15:41.494 --> 00:15:43.410
all of the stuff that
we're putting out there,

00:15:43.410 --> 00:15:44.610
it's all on GitHub.

00:15:44.610 --> 00:15:48.120
So you can actually go to
github.com/googlecloudplatform,

00:15:48.120 --> 00:15:50.520
search for .NET, and you're
going to find all the source

00:15:50.520 --> 00:15:52.812
code that we used to go
build all of this stuff.

00:15:52.812 --> 00:15:54.270
And of course, the
part that I want

00:15:54.270 --> 00:15:55.430
to take a little
bit of time and look

00:15:55.430 --> 00:15:57.054
at that I think is
the most interesting

00:15:57.054 --> 00:16:00.330
is the integration directly
into Visual Studio.

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:03.700
So let's take a look
and try that out.

00:16:03.700 --> 00:16:06.410
All right, so over here,
I'm running Visual Studio.

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:08.780
This is-- I believe it's 2015.

00:16:08.780 --> 00:16:13.620
And I've got a hello world
ASP.NET project up and running.

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:17.930
So I'm going to go ahead and
Alt-Tab over to my web browser.

00:16:17.930 --> 00:16:19.610
You can see it's
running on localhost.

00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:21.350
And this is your traditional--
for those of you that have done

00:16:21.350 --> 00:16:23.683
ASP.NET, you're probably used
to staring at this screen.

00:16:23.683 --> 00:16:26.670
It's the traditional file, new
project, hello world template.

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:28.340
And what you can
do, as of now, is

00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:29.450
if you go-- if I want
to-- let's say I'm

00:16:29.450 --> 00:16:30.950
ready to deploy to App Engine.

00:16:30.950 --> 00:16:32.720
I can go to my project file.

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:34.040
I can right-click on that.

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:35.450
And I'm going get a
couple of new options,

00:16:35.450 --> 00:16:36.908
but the one that
we're going to try

00:16:36.908 --> 00:16:38.979
is Publish hellogcp
to Google Cloud.

00:16:38.979 --> 00:16:41.270
Now, at this point, we're
going to give you the option.

00:16:41.270 --> 00:16:42.290
We're actually going
to let you choose,

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:44.664
do you want to deploy to App
Engine flexible environment.

00:16:44.664 --> 00:16:46.850
Do you want to deploy
to Container Engine?

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:48.766
And we're also going to
be rolling out support

00:16:48.766 --> 00:16:50.900
for doing this on Compute
Engine soon as well.

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:52.358
And you can just
pick one of these.

00:16:52.358 --> 00:16:54.442
And wherever you want to
deploy, click the button.

00:16:54.442 --> 00:16:56.191
Tell us what you want
to name the version.

00:16:56.191 --> 00:16:57.830
We automatically
provision one for you.

00:16:57.830 --> 00:16:59.360
We're going to automatically
promote so that this

00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:00.377
goes straight to prod.

00:17:00.377 --> 00:17:02.960
And when it's done, I want it
to go ahead and open the website

00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:04.550
and show me the site.

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:05.990
So as this is going
off right now,

00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:06.800
we're going to do the build.

00:17:06.800 --> 00:17:08.750
It's going to kick off
the deployment process.

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:10.624
And while that happens,
I want to take a look

00:17:10.624 --> 00:17:12.480
at a couple of other things.

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:16.640
So now, under Tools, we also
have this Google Cloud Tools,

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:19.469
and we have the Cloud Explorer
and the Account Manager.

00:17:19.469 --> 00:17:21.010
Let's check out the
Account Explorer.

00:17:21.010 --> 00:17:22.550
This is kind of
my favorite part.

00:17:22.550 --> 00:17:24.147
From within-- inside
of Visual Studio,

00:17:24.147 --> 00:17:26.480
without having to go to the
Google Developer-- the Cloud

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:29.120
Developer-- console, I can get
a list of all of my App Engine

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:31.890
applications, each version
that's out there running.

00:17:31.890 --> 00:17:34.310
You can see I already deployed
this version earlier today.

00:17:34.310 --> 00:17:36.110
I can see all of my
Google Compute Engine

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:37.370
virtual machines.

00:17:37.370 --> 00:17:39.190
And I have options to
interact with those

00:17:39.190 --> 00:17:40.640
and set settings on them.

00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:42.290
I have all my Cloud
Storage buckets

00:17:42.290 --> 00:17:44.300
and all of my Google
Cloud SQL instances

00:17:44.300 --> 00:17:46.250
all right here inside
of Visual Studio.

00:17:46.250 --> 00:17:48.500
If you're a .NET developer,
we think this is something

00:17:48.500 --> 00:17:49.720
you're really going to love.

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:51.470
So while we're waiting
for this to deploy,

00:17:51.470 --> 00:17:53.780
you can see I already have
a version deployed out here.

00:17:53.780 --> 00:17:56.480
Let's go ahead and check it
out running on Google Cloud.

00:17:56.480 --> 00:18:00.650
So I can right-click and say
Open the Running Service.

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:03.345
And there we have it,
ASP.NET on App Engine.

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:15.220
All right, so trick number three
was running .NET on App Engine.

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:17.280
Let's get into
trick number four.

00:18:17.280 --> 00:18:20.340
Trick number four is, I can
customize my operating system

00:18:20.340 --> 00:18:20.970
packages.

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:23.541
Now, what does this mean,
and why is it as important?

00:18:23.541 --> 00:18:25.290
Traditionally, with
Platform as a Service,

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:27.270
one of the walls
that we've run into

00:18:27.270 --> 00:18:29.670
is that you'll get started,
you deploy your hello world,

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:32.040
your hello ASP.NET
web application,

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:35.220
you get up to a certain
point, and you hit a wall.

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:36.580
There's a library you need.

00:18:36.580 --> 00:18:38.144
There is a language
you want to use.

00:18:38.144 --> 00:18:39.810
There's some part of
the system that you

00:18:39.810 --> 00:18:41.700
need to go beyond where, if
you were on a virtual machine,

00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:42.440
you could do it.

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:44.790
But because you're in a
Platform as a Service offering,

00:18:44.790 --> 00:18:45.660
you get stuck.

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:47.160
Well, we don't think
you should have

00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:48.575
to get stuck when that happens.

00:18:48.575 --> 00:18:49.950
So what we want
to do is give you

00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:52.290
the ability to bring in any
operating system package,

00:18:52.290 --> 00:18:55.710
and your Linux virtual machine,
that you want to bring in.

00:18:55.710 --> 00:18:57.956
And so as a way to show
this off, what I want to do

00:18:57.956 --> 00:18:59.580
is talk about, there's
this application

00:18:59.580 --> 00:19:00.360
that I wanted to build.

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:02.220
I wanted to send everybody
home with something.

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:04.830
And as it would turn out, that
would have been a lot of durian

00:19:04.830 --> 00:19:06.120
if I'd have chosen that.

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:08.290
So instead, I want to send you
all home with a Twitter card.

00:19:08.290 --> 00:19:09.960
And this is a card you
can make on your own.

00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:11.085
It's got the Next branding.

00:19:11.085 --> 00:19:11.940
Take a picture.

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:14.160
And you can go ahead
and put that on Twitter

00:19:14.160 --> 00:19:15.884
if you'd like to.

00:19:15.884 --> 00:19:17.300
So let's see what
this looks like.

00:19:17.300 --> 00:19:20.040
Let's actually go ahead
and give it a try.

00:19:20.040 --> 00:19:23.800
So I'm going to go
ahead to appengine.io.

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:25.550
And what this is going
to do is it's going

00:19:25.550 --> 00:19:27.390
to use the webcam inside--

00:19:27.390 --> 00:19:28.480
on the web page.

00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:31.610
I'm going to give a smile.

00:19:31.610 --> 00:19:32.300
All right.

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:33.860
And this is going to go take--

00:19:33.860 --> 00:19:37.130
send my picture to App Engine,
use a couple of custom tools,

00:19:37.130 --> 00:19:38.060
generate this card.

00:19:38.060 --> 00:19:41.210
And then when I'm ready,
I can go tweet it out--

00:19:41.210 --> 00:19:41.835
so pretty cool.

00:19:41.835 --> 00:19:43.834
So how would I go and
build something like this?

00:19:43.834 --> 00:19:45.293
How do I do the
image manipulation?

00:19:45.293 --> 00:19:47.917
This is a very classic wall when
you're running up and building

00:19:47.917 --> 00:19:48.860
one of these apps.

00:19:48.860 --> 00:19:50.990
The way that I was able
to do it on my MacBook

00:19:50.990 --> 00:19:54.200
is I just ran brew install
imagemagick, you know?

00:19:54.200 --> 00:19:54.700
Done.

00:19:54.700 --> 00:19:55.410
It was amazing.

00:19:55.410 --> 00:19:56.368
It works on my machine.

00:19:56.368 --> 00:19:57.434
Ship it.

00:19:57.434 --> 00:19:59.600
And then I went a little
further, and I figured out,

00:19:59.600 --> 00:20:01.350
actually, I want
GraphicsMagick, you know,

00:20:01.350 --> 00:20:02.810
that sits on top of ImageMagick.

00:20:02.810 --> 00:20:05.000
OK, so now I'm building
up a lot of libraries.

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:06.260
Well, it worked locally.

00:20:06.260 --> 00:20:10.910
But how do I get
that onto App Engine?

00:20:10.910 --> 00:20:13.665
And this is where Docker
comes to the rescue.

00:20:13.665 --> 00:20:15.290
And let's actually
check it out and see

00:20:15.290 --> 00:20:18.300
how we would go build that.

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:21.210
So here is my local version
of the web application.

00:20:21.210 --> 00:20:23.700
This happens to be in
Node.js by the way,

00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:26.790
but you could run it in
whatever language you'd like to.

00:20:26.790 --> 00:20:28.740
And if you look
down, I'm going to go

00:20:28.740 --> 00:20:30.810
find the spot where I'm
grabbing the image that

00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:32.070
gets posted to my site.

00:20:32.070 --> 00:20:34.650
So it's posting to
this sendpic endpoint.

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:37.050
And then I'm going to
save the file locally.

00:20:37.050 --> 00:20:39.960
And then I'm going to go
use the GraphicsMagick npm

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:44.400
module, which has a dependency
on that underlying binary being

00:20:44.400 --> 00:20:46.380
installed inside
of my container.

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:48.000
So how do I do this?

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:49.800
Well, what I'm going
to do right now

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:51.600
is go out to the command line.

00:20:51.600 --> 00:20:55.060
And I'm going to go ahead into
my Photobooth application.

00:20:55.060 --> 00:20:56.987
You can see all
of our files here.

00:20:56.987 --> 00:20:59.070
And what I want to do is
I want to grab the Docker

00:20:59.070 --> 00:21:01.050
file that App Engine
is using under the hood

00:21:01.050 --> 00:21:01.890
to build my system.

00:21:01.890 --> 00:21:04.260
Right now, the only thing that
I've had to tell the App Engine

00:21:04.260 --> 00:21:05.330
is, here's an app.yaml.

00:21:05.330 --> 00:21:07.350
Please use Node.js for me.

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:09.210
But I want to control
the Docker file.

00:21:09.210 --> 00:21:13.310
So what I can do now is
I can say gcloud beta app

00:21:13.310 --> 00:21:14.900
gen-config --custom.

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:19.810
We're going to tell it
to update my app.yaml.

00:21:19.810 --> 00:21:21.570
And now, when I
go back over here,

00:21:21.570 --> 00:21:23.500
I'm going to get a
couple of new files.

00:21:23.500 --> 00:21:25.875
I'm going to get this Docker
file, which is a file that I

00:21:25.875 --> 00:21:26.727
didn't have before.

00:21:26.727 --> 00:21:28.310
And it really explains
how we're going

00:21:28.310 --> 00:21:29.770
to build your application.

00:21:29.770 --> 00:21:31.850
I'm going to use the
base Node.js image--

00:21:31.850 --> 00:21:33.795
again, open source on GitHub.

00:21:33.795 --> 00:21:36.170
And we're going to go ahead
and install our dependencies.

00:21:36.170 --> 00:21:37.340
I happen to use Yarn.

00:21:37.340 --> 00:21:39.140
If you're a Node.js
person, then you

00:21:39.140 --> 00:21:41.644
know it's kind of the
hipster thing to do now.

00:21:41.644 --> 00:21:43.060
So what we would
need to do now is

00:21:43.060 --> 00:21:44.260
we need to add the
part of code that's

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:45.843
going to install our
client libraries.

00:21:45.843 --> 00:21:47.240
So let's give this a shot.

00:21:47.240 --> 00:21:52.690
So what I'm going to have to
say RUN apt-get install -y--

00:21:52.690 --> 00:21:54.160
ooh, I almost messed it up--

00:21:54.160 --> 00:21:57.100
apt-get update-- update--

00:21:57.100 --> 00:22:06.580
and then apt-get install -y -q
imagemagick and graphicsmagick.

00:22:06.580 --> 00:22:08.710
Save, and that's it.

00:22:08.710 --> 00:22:10.450
When you take and
gcloud app deploy this,

00:22:10.450 --> 00:22:12.820
because there's this Docker
file in my current working

00:22:12.820 --> 00:22:14.770
directory, it's going
to take that as a cue.

00:22:14.770 --> 00:22:16.372
And instead of
using the container

00:22:16.372 --> 00:22:17.830
that we were going
to pop out, it's

00:22:17.830 --> 00:22:21.470
going to use the base container
and add our stuff on top of it.

00:22:21.470 --> 00:22:23.410
And then if I want to
try this out locally,

00:22:23.410 --> 00:22:26.350
I really even need anything
quite like a local emulator.

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:28.630
I can actually just use the
standard Docker toolchain

00:22:28.630 --> 00:22:30.920
to test this out and know
that it's going to work.

00:22:30.920 --> 00:22:36.480
So I can go off and do
my docker build -t myapp.

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:38.507
And I'm going to end up
with a container that

00:22:38.507 --> 00:22:40.090
has everything I
need, at which point,

00:22:40.090 --> 00:22:41.640
I can go ahead
and docker run it.

00:22:41.640 --> 00:22:43.520
When I'm ready to go
to production again,

00:22:43.520 --> 00:22:45.330
gcloud app deploy.

00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:47.630
And it's going to
be off to the races.

00:22:50.710 --> 00:22:53.782
So again, we were able to
just use gcloud app deploy.

00:22:53.782 --> 00:22:55.740
We were able to build
our own Docker container.

00:22:55.740 --> 00:22:57.656
All we had to do was add
a little bit of logic

00:22:57.656 --> 00:22:59.457
to install ImageMagick
and GraphicsMagick.

00:22:59.457 --> 00:23:01.540
And again, this is one of
those times-- everybody,

00:23:01.540 --> 00:23:05.010
get out your phone, or
your laptop, or whatever.

00:23:05.010 --> 00:23:07.920
You can go to
card.appengine.io and actually

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:10.510
tweet out the card that
you're generating from this.

00:23:10.510 --> 00:23:12.350
And I'm going to go
ahead and do it as well.

00:23:12.350 --> 00:23:15.530
I didn't actually tweet out
the last one, so let's do that.

00:23:15.530 --> 00:23:18.050
Or here, I'll actually
tweet this out.

00:23:18.050 --> 00:23:20.130
So there we go.

00:23:20.130 --> 00:23:22.260
Sweet.

00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:22.980
Make a good face.

00:23:22.980 --> 00:23:26.070
Make it look happy, people.

00:23:26.070 --> 00:23:28.440
All right.

00:23:28.440 --> 00:23:31.050
So trick number four, we were
able to customize the operating

00:23:31.050 --> 00:23:32.280
system packages.

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:35.220
Trick number five, we're
actually going to take,

00:23:35.220 --> 00:23:37.404
and we're going to make our
app a little bit better.

00:23:37.404 --> 00:23:39.570
So when you look at the
application that I just ran,

00:23:39.570 --> 00:23:41.490
it was really a single
App Engine service.

00:23:41.490 --> 00:23:43.500
And it was a bit of
a monolith, right?

00:23:43.500 --> 00:23:46.260
We had one code base that
was responsible for accepting

00:23:46.260 --> 00:23:47.240
the uploads.

00:23:47.240 --> 00:23:48.750
It was running image processing.

00:23:48.750 --> 00:23:51.920
It was generating new images,
writing things to GCS.

00:23:51.920 --> 00:23:52.980
It was sending out links.

00:23:52.980 --> 00:23:55.740
You know, this was one
service that was doing a lot.

00:23:55.740 --> 00:23:57.840
And you have to
wonder, over time,

00:23:57.840 --> 00:23:59.700
what's going to happen
when I scale up?

00:23:59.700 --> 00:24:02.802
What if my web process
needs fewer resources

00:24:02.802 --> 00:24:05.010
than the parts that's doing
image manipulation, which

00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:06.979
is most likely the case, right?

00:24:06.979 --> 00:24:08.520
Is my application
going to be scaling

00:24:08.520 --> 00:24:09.967
as efficiently as it could be?

00:24:09.967 --> 00:24:11.550
What happens when
I'm ready to deploy?

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:13.129
I've introduced more risk.

00:24:13.129 --> 00:24:15.420
Every part of my application
is all bound in one thing,

00:24:15.420 --> 00:24:16.810
and I can't separate it out.

00:24:16.810 --> 00:24:18.900
So instead of just having one
service that's having problems

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:20.430
and deploying one
set of changes,

00:24:20.430 --> 00:24:22.305
I have to bring the
whole thing up and put it

00:24:22.305 --> 00:24:24.340
all out at the same time.

00:24:24.340 --> 00:24:27.030
So the next thing that you can--
trick you can use on App Engine

00:24:27.030 --> 00:24:29.280
is building apps that
use microservice-style

00:24:29.280 --> 00:24:31.760
architectures.

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:34.010
So the way this works
is, on App Engine,

00:24:34.010 --> 00:24:35.660
every time I gcloud
app deploy, I

00:24:35.660 --> 00:24:37.460
can choose to build
another service.

00:24:37.460 --> 00:24:39.293
And I can keep adding
more and more of these

00:24:39.293 --> 00:24:40.730
to construct my application.

00:24:40.730 --> 00:24:43.910
These services can talk to each
other directly through HTTP,

00:24:43.910 --> 00:24:45.960
or they can use a
worker queue system.

00:24:45.960 --> 00:24:48.180
In my case, what I did
is I took the front end,

00:24:48.180 --> 00:24:50.510
I separated it out into
a front end process.

00:24:50.510 --> 00:24:52.970
It's only responsible
for serving web traffic

00:24:52.970 --> 00:24:54.950
and accepting
uploads of the image.

00:24:54.950 --> 00:24:58.700
I then created a backend service
with a different autoscaler

00:24:58.700 --> 00:25:00.380
and its own set
of instances that

00:25:00.380 --> 00:25:03.440
are responsible for
taking images, running it

00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:05.800
through GraphicsMagick, and
then sending out the links

00:25:05.800 --> 00:25:07.550
so that they get
rendered in the page.

00:25:07.550 --> 00:25:10.250
And then I connect the two
using a worker queue pattern.

00:25:10.250 --> 00:25:12.350
And I'm using Google
Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:25:12.350 --> 00:25:14.690
So by doing this, what I'm
able to do is independently

00:25:14.690 --> 00:25:16.562
scale the front end
of my application

00:25:16.562 --> 00:25:18.020
from the back end
of my application

00:25:18.020 --> 00:25:20.570
making sure that I'm only
using the resources that I need

00:25:20.570 --> 00:25:21.980
and reducing the
amount of risk I

00:25:21.980 --> 00:25:24.620
have when I roll
out new changes.

00:25:24.620 --> 00:25:26.750
So to do this, we saw
that file, app.yaml,

00:25:26.750 --> 00:25:30.920
which essentially described how
I want App Engine to interpret

00:25:30.920 --> 00:25:32.390
the code that we're sending it.

00:25:32.390 --> 00:25:34.640
And what I had to tell it
was, the runtime is Node,

00:25:34.640 --> 00:25:37.190
the environment is the App
Engine flexible environment,

00:25:37.190 --> 00:25:40.160
and the name of my service
is the frontend service.

00:25:40.160 --> 00:25:42.980
And I had one of these
for the back end as well.

00:25:42.980 --> 00:25:45.590
And then when you're ready, all
you do is gcloud app deploy.

00:25:45.590 --> 00:25:48.200
And you can run many of these
within the same project using

00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:50.870
the same underlying resources.

00:25:50.870 --> 00:25:53.630
But in this example, I used
Node, because at this stage,

00:25:53.630 --> 00:25:56.280
JavaScript is the language
I'm the most comfortable with.

00:25:56.280 --> 00:25:57.860
What if you don't want to
use Node for your back end?

00:25:57.860 --> 00:25:59.570
What if it's a different
team building the back end

00:25:59.570 --> 00:26:00.630
than the front end?

00:26:00.630 --> 00:26:02.390
What if you want to use Perl?

00:26:05.210 --> 00:26:10.191
Well, that happens sometimes.

00:26:10.191 --> 00:26:12.440
Sometimes you have an old
piece of code laying around.

00:26:12.440 --> 00:26:14.060
Or you have that
guy on your team,

00:26:14.060 --> 00:26:16.905
or that girl on your team, who
just needs to do it in Perl.

00:26:16.905 --> 00:26:18.530
And on App Engine,
this was another one

00:26:18.530 --> 00:26:21.390
of those walls of pahs
that you would run into,

00:26:21.390 --> 00:26:23.130
but not anymore.

00:26:23.130 --> 00:26:25.820
So the next trick that
you can do on App Engine--

00:26:25.820 --> 00:26:27.320
number five was
microservice-style

00:26:27.320 --> 00:26:28.370
architectures.

00:26:28.370 --> 00:26:31.070
Number six is bring your own
language to the platform--

00:26:31.070 --> 00:26:33.560
yes, even Perl.

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:34.800
So let's try it.

00:26:34.800 --> 00:26:37.020
Let's do it.

00:26:37.020 --> 00:26:37.800
All right.

00:26:37.800 --> 00:26:38.883
I'm going to go over here.

00:26:38.883 --> 00:26:41.010
I'm going to get out of
my Photobooth application.

00:26:41.010 --> 00:26:45.270
And I'm going to go into some
demo Perl code that I wrote.

00:26:45.270 --> 00:26:46.690
And let's take a look at that.

00:26:49.730 --> 00:26:53.090
All right, so here I have a
very simple Perl-based web

00:26:53.090 --> 00:26:55.190
application.

00:26:55.190 --> 00:26:57.350
It's, I believe, Perl 5.

00:26:57.350 --> 00:26:59.960
It's using a web framework
called Mojolicious,

00:26:59.960 --> 00:27:02.130
which is fun to say.

00:27:02.130 --> 00:27:04.130
And it's essentially just
a very simple web app.

00:27:04.130 --> 00:27:06.020
It has a single handler
that's going off

00:27:06.020 --> 00:27:07.700
on the root of the
web application.

00:27:07.700 --> 00:27:09.590
And it's going to
render a template named

00:27:09.590 --> 00:27:10.744
"index" that I give it.

00:27:10.744 --> 00:27:12.410
And over here in my
templates directory,

00:27:12.410 --> 00:27:15.350
I have template.html.ep.

00:27:15.350 --> 00:27:17.270
And it just says,
"Welcome to Perl,"

00:27:17.270 --> 00:27:19.510
along with a fancy
green background color.

00:27:19.510 --> 00:27:21.260
And then, to make that
work on App Engine,

00:27:21.260 --> 00:27:23.880
I really only needed
to add two files.

00:27:23.880 --> 00:27:26.225
So one is I had to add
this app.yaml file, which

00:27:26.225 --> 00:27:27.350
we've already talked about.

00:27:27.350 --> 00:27:29.810
This describes how, to App
Engine-- tells App Engine

00:27:29.810 --> 00:27:30.890
how to run the app.

00:27:30.890 --> 00:27:32.630
We say, it's a custom runtime.

00:27:32.630 --> 00:27:33.530
Don't use Node.js.

00:27:33.530 --> 00:27:34.640
Don't use Ruby.

00:27:34.640 --> 00:27:37.370
I'm going to tell you how
to go run this application.

00:27:37.370 --> 00:27:38.387
We have the environment.

00:27:38.387 --> 00:27:40.470
And then, I'm deploying
multiple of these services

00:27:40.470 --> 00:27:42.303
within one app, because
we're actually going

00:27:42.303 --> 00:27:44.300
to try a few other languages.

00:27:44.300 --> 00:27:46.280
And then, the way that
we do a custom runtime

00:27:46.280 --> 00:27:48.110
is by providing our
own Docker file.

00:27:48.110 --> 00:27:50.660
The exact same way that we
were able to customize Node,

00:27:50.660 --> 00:27:53.300
we can use that to
bring our own language.

00:27:53.300 --> 00:27:56.120
And so in this case, instead of
using the built in App Engine

00:27:56.120 --> 00:27:58.160
base image in Docker,
I'm just going

00:27:58.160 --> 00:28:00.170
to use the latest version
of the Perl base image

00:28:00.170 --> 00:28:01.970
that's out on Docker hub.

00:28:01.970 --> 00:28:03.996
I'm going to install
some dependencies,

00:28:03.996 --> 00:28:05.870
so I'm going to use
Perl's package management

00:28:05.870 --> 00:28:09.710
system to go install
the Mojolicious library.

00:28:09.710 --> 00:28:11.692
I'm going to tell it to
listen on port 8080--

00:28:11.692 --> 00:28:14.150
this is a bit of a magic port
on App Engine that says where

00:28:14.150 --> 00:28:16.420
your application is
supposed to serve--

00:28:16.420 --> 00:28:19.610
add the source files, play
around with permissions,

00:28:19.610 --> 00:28:22.430
and then finally, run
the main Perl process.

00:28:22.430 --> 00:28:23.690
And that's it.

00:28:23.690 --> 00:28:25.820
I can take this, run
gcloud app deploy,

00:28:25.820 --> 00:28:29.025
and have a live version
of Perl running on my app.

00:28:29.025 --> 00:28:29.900
But let's try it out.

00:28:29.900 --> 00:28:31.950
Let's see what actually happens.

00:28:31.950 --> 00:28:34.550
So I deployed this, like
most of these, earlier today.

00:28:34.550 --> 00:28:38.900
And I'm going to go ahead and
do a gcloud app services list,

00:28:38.900 --> 00:28:41.000
give you a little
preview, maybe, of things

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:41.930
that are to come.

00:28:41.930 --> 00:28:44.892
We can see, one of them
here is the Perl service.

00:28:44.892 --> 00:28:46.850
And just to see this
running out on App Engine,

00:28:46.850 --> 00:28:50.885
I can do gcloud app
browse --service perl.

