00:34:50.073 --> 00:34:51.989
But let's talk about how
I actually built that

00:34:51.989 --> 00:34:54.690
and why this might put
a strain on my system.

00:34:54.690 --> 00:34:57.090
So to build this, I
chose to use FFmpeg,

00:34:57.090 --> 00:34:58.590
which is kind of
like the Swiss army

00:34:58.590 --> 00:35:00.030
knife of video editing
in the same way

00:35:00.030 --> 00:35:01.470
that ImageMagick
is the Swiss army

00:35:01.470 --> 00:35:03.870
knife for image processing.

00:35:03.870 --> 00:35:06.290
But as you could imagine,
when I started playing around

00:35:06.290 --> 00:35:08.790
with that, especially if any
of you are trying it right now,

00:35:08.790 --> 00:35:11.880
FFmpeg takes a fair
bit of CPU cycles.

00:35:11.880 --> 00:35:13.970
This is video processing.

00:35:13.970 --> 00:35:17.180
It tends to be very
CPU-intensive work.

00:35:17.180 --> 00:35:19.680
So that leads us to trick number
seven of what you can build

00:35:19.680 --> 00:35:22.260
on App Engine, which is, you
can actually-- because it's

00:35:22.260 --> 00:35:25.170
so CPU-intensive, I want
to customize the underlying

00:35:25.170 --> 00:35:26.010
hardware.

00:35:26.010 --> 00:35:27.509
And this is a new
thing that you can

00:35:27.509 --> 00:35:30.804
do on top of App Engine
in GA as of this week.

00:35:30.804 --> 00:35:32.220
To do that, we're
taking advantage

00:35:32.220 --> 00:35:35.359
of Google Compute Engine's
custom machine type feature.

00:35:35.359 --> 00:35:36.900
Now, what this lets
us do is actually

00:35:36.900 --> 00:35:40.380
size our instances not based
on a pre-canned set of machine

00:35:40.380 --> 00:35:42.810
types, but size our
instance completely tailored

00:35:42.810 --> 00:35:44.290
to our needs.

00:35:44.290 --> 00:35:46.740
So right now, we give you
a preset version of CPU,

00:35:46.740 --> 00:35:49.210
memory, and disk that seems
to work for most people.

00:35:49.210 --> 00:35:52.020
But for my image-- or for my
video processing application,

00:35:52.020 --> 00:35:54.270
what I really needed is, I
didn't need as much memory.

00:35:54.270 --> 00:35:56.700
When it came to disk, I wasn't
storing anything locally.

00:35:56.700 --> 00:35:59.400
I was using Google
Cloud Storage.

00:35:59.400 --> 00:36:00.750
So I didn't need much disk.

00:36:00.750 --> 00:36:03.680
When it came to CPU,
I needed a lot of CPU

00:36:03.680 --> 00:36:05.610
if we were going to
handle a couple of people

00:36:05.610 --> 00:36:07.990
in a room for this talk.

00:36:07.990 --> 00:36:09.870
And to specify that,
all you need to do

00:36:09.870 --> 00:36:12.510
is, inside of that app.yaml
file where you're telling us

00:36:12.510 --> 00:36:14.670
the rest of how to build
your app, just tell us,

00:36:14.670 --> 00:36:17.290
I need a lot of CPU, I need
a little bit of memory.

00:36:17.290 --> 00:36:18.210
And that's it.

00:36:18.210 --> 00:36:20.220
You can customize
the hardware inside

00:36:20.220 --> 00:36:22.440
of your machine completely
based on the needs

00:36:22.440 --> 00:36:24.950
of your application.

00:36:24.950 --> 00:36:26.169
So let's give it a try.

00:36:26.169 --> 00:36:28.210
And if you want to give
it-- we already did this,

00:36:28.210 --> 00:36:29.585
but if you want
to give it a try,

00:36:29.585 --> 00:36:31.550
you can go out to
gif.appengine.io.

00:36:31.550 --> 00:36:33.679
We can see if we can get
it to scale up at the end,

00:36:33.679 --> 00:36:34.970
get it to scale all the way up.

00:36:34.970 --> 00:36:36.520
And at the end, we can
take a look at the stats.

00:36:36.520 --> 00:36:38.186
You have to prime
some statistics for me

00:36:38.186 --> 00:36:40.480
for the dashboard.

00:36:40.480 --> 00:36:42.685
All right, so we're getting
towards the end now,

00:36:42.685 --> 00:36:43.810
and we've got seven tricks.

00:36:43.810 --> 00:36:46.018
Our last one was customizing
the underlying hardware.

00:36:46.018 --> 00:36:47.470
It was 7 or 8.

00:36:47.470 --> 00:36:49.190
We got the last one to work.

00:36:49.190 --> 00:36:50.830
I called on an old
demo from last year,

00:36:50.830 --> 00:36:52.574
actually, to get
this put together.

00:36:52.574 --> 00:36:54.490
But if you look back
through our application--

00:36:54.490 --> 00:36:56.489
I think I've used this
slide a couple of times--

00:36:56.489 --> 00:36:57.769
we had a pretty simple app.

00:36:57.769 --> 00:37:00.310
But you know, it was a pretty
trivial app that we just built,

00:37:00.310 --> 00:37:00.730
right?

00:37:00.730 --> 00:37:02.938
There were two App Engine
services, one Pub/Sub thing

00:37:02.938 --> 00:37:03.690
in the middle.

00:37:03.690 --> 00:37:05.830
In production, our
apps rarely end up

00:37:05.830 --> 00:37:07.000
actually looking like that.

00:37:07.000 --> 00:37:09.059
This is kind of a
contrived example.

00:37:09.059 --> 00:37:11.350
A more classic example of
what you're going to go build

00:37:11.350 --> 00:37:13.540
is something that looks
like this, you know?

00:37:13.540 --> 00:37:14.380
We're going to have front ends.

00:37:14.380 --> 00:37:14.860
We're going to have back ends.

00:37:14.860 --> 00:37:15.940
We're going to have the queues.

00:37:15.940 --> 00:37:17.930
We're going to have APIs that
we're taking advantage of.

00:37:17.930 --> 00:37:19.300
We're going to have
scalable file storage.

00:37:19.300 --> 00:37:20.380
We're going to have databases.

00:37:20.380 --> 00:37:21.490
We're going to have
managed databases.

00:37:21.490 --> 00:37:23.260
We're going to have
virtual machines that

00:37:23.260 --> 00:37:25.480
run that old piece of software
that we couldn't port.

00:37:25.480 --> 00:37:27.070
You know, there are all
kinds of different pieces

00:37:27.070 --> 00:37:29.195
in a real production service,
along with duct tape,

00:37:29.195 --> 00:37:31.960
and baling wire, and
love, that it takes

00:37:31.960 --> 00:37:34.490
to keep it running over time.

00:37:34.490 --> 00:37:37.300
So to do this, one of the
best parts about App Engine

00:37:37.300 --> 00:37:40.630
is that you have access
to all of the APIs

00:37:40.630 --> 00:37:42.700
that are available
inside of Google Cloud.

00:37:42.700 --> 00:37:45.340
Spanner, we announced,
Cloud Machine Learning,

00:37:45.340 --> 00:37:47.669
the Video Intelligence
API, Pub/Sub, BigQuery,

00:37:47.669 --> 00:37:49.210
Vision API-- there's
a big, long list

00:37:49.210 --> 00:37:50.831
of all the different things.

00:37:50.831 --> 00:37:52.330
These are now all
at your fingertips

00:37:52.330 --> 00:37:55.120
and simple to use from within
of your App Engine application.

00:37:55.120 --> 00:37:57.730
You don't just have to use
the services that come out

00:37:57.730 --> 00:37:59.230
of the box with App Engine.

00:37:59.230 --> 00:38:00.460
It's all of GCP.

00:38:00.460 --> 00:38:04.400
GCP is the new
Platform as a Service.

00:38:04.400 --> 00:38:06.350
So to do that on
Node.js, all I had to do

00:38:06.350 --> 00:38:08.380
was npm install
google-cloud, and we

00:38:08.380 --> 00:38:11.530
had an npm module that had
access to all those services.

00:38:11.530 --> 00:38:13.030
Of course, if you're
in Python, pip.

00:38:13.030 --> 00:38:14.129
If you're in Ruby, gem.

00:38:14.129 --> 00:38:16.420
And I kept going with this,
but it was too many slides,

00:38:16.420 --> 00:38:16.720
you know?

00:38:16.720 --> 00:38:18.640
It's available on all
of those seven languages

00:38:18.640 --> 00:38:20.476
that we talked about earlier.

00:38:20.476 --> 00:38:22.350
And you use this API,
let's say that I wanted

00:38:22.350 --> 00:38:24.020
to do something very simple.

00:38:24.020 --> 00:38:26.650
Let's say that I just wanted
to be able to look at a picture

00:38:26.650 --> 00:38:28.600
and figure out what's
in that picture--

00:38:28.600 --> 00:38:30.635
traditionally, one of
those problems that

00:38:30.635 --> 00:38:32.290
take an army of
computer scientists

00:38:32.290 --> 00:38:33.950
eight years to solve.

00:38:33.950 --> 00:38:37.575
Well, with Google Cloud, on App
Engine, using the Vision API,

00:38:37.575 --> 00:38:40.740
I can do it in four
lines of code--

00:38:40.740 --> 00:38:42.700
well, four lines of JavaScript.

00:38:42.700 --> 00:38:44.650
I can do it in four
lines of JavaScript.

00:38:44.650 --> 00:38:47.620
We just bring in the
library, detect the labels.

00:38:47.620 --> 00:38:50.410
And then we can find out, is
it a dog, a canine, a poodle.

00:38:50.410 --> 00:38:52.990
But you don't have to
take my word for it.

00:38:52.990 --> 00:38:54.490
We're going to try it out.

00:38:54.490 --> 00:38:56.795
So with access to these
machine learning APIs,

00:38:56.795 --> 00:38:58.420
all this technology
that we've built up

00:38:58.420 --> 00:39:00.850
at Google over the
last two decades,

00:39:00.850 --> 00:39:03.370
all the intelligence, all
the stuff in Google Photos,

00:39:03.370 --> 00:39:04.930
mail, inbox--

00:39:04.930 --> 00:39:07.519
you know, amazing technology
now at our fingertips--

00:39:07.519 --> 00:39:10.060
we could actually go off, and
we could change the world if we

00:39:10.060 --> 00:39:12.770
wanted to with this stuff.

00:39:12.770 --> 00:39:16.290
I used it to build
an app about my cat.

00:39:16.290 --> 00:39:20.230
So let's see what
that looks like.

00:39:20.230 --> 00:39:23.140
So I'm going to go to
cloudcats.appspot.com.

00:39:23.140 --> 00:39:25.950
And what I wanted
to find out is I

00:39:25.950 --> 00:39:29.430
wanted to find out if people
found dogs or cats to be

00:39:29.430 --> 00:39:30.490
the cutest of animals.

00:39:30.490 --> 00:39:33.360
And to do that, I decided
to use the Cloud Vision API.

00:39:33.360 --> 00:39:34.860
So what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:39:34.860 --> 00:39:36.840
to go off and read a
bunch of data from Reddit,

00:39:36.840 --> 00:39:38.924
r/aww to be specific.

00:39:38.924 --> 00:39:39.840
This is all real time.

00:39:39.840 --> 00:39:41.500
You can go and check
it out if you want to.

00:39:41.500 --> 00:39:43.374
And we're going to figure
out, are there more

00:39:43.374 --> 00:39:47.040
pictures of dogs or cats
on the aww subreddit.

00:39:47.040 --> 00:39:49.330
So let's give it a shot.

00:39:49.330 --> 00:39:52.020
OK, so we're downloading
each of these images.

00:39:52.020 --> 00:39:53.300
This is a front end process.

00:39:53.300 --> 00:39:54.380
It has a back end.

00:39:54.380 --> 00:39:56.420
Communication is
through Pub/Sub.

00:39:56.420 --> 00:39:57.912
And it's using the Video API.

00:39:57.912 --> 00:39:59.120
We've got our first response.

00:39:59.120 --> 00:40:02.000
And it looks like we've got
cats out to an early lead,

00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:03.767
but the dogs are catching up.

00:40:03.767 --> 00:40:05.600
Oh, there are a lot
more puppies in this one

00:40:05.600 --> 00:40:07.224
than there usually
are, so that's good.

00:40:07.224 --> 00:40:08.652
We've got sleepy kitty.

00:40:08.652 --> 00:40:10.610
We've got a little bit
of duplication going on,

00:40:10.610 --> 00:40:12.170
but that's OK.

00:40:12.170 --> 00:40:14.510
I think the dogs are
back out to a lead.

00:40:14.510 --> 00:40:16.160
We have a-- I think
that's a Yoda cat.

00:40:16.160 --> 00:40:18.170
That's awesome.

00:40:18.170 --> 00:40:22.705
We've got Jedi cat,
dog in sunglasses.

00:40:22.705 --> 00:40:24.330
And this is going to
go on for a while.

00:40:24.330 --> 00:40:25.245
And at the end of
this, we're going

00:40:25.245 --> 00:40:26.720
to be able to use
the Cloud Vision

00:40:26.720 --> 00:40:29.012
API to break up
and split out all

00:40:29.012 --> 00:40:30.470
of these different
characteristics.

00:40:30.470 --> 00:40:32.136
You know, all the
chaos in these images,

00:40:32.136 --> 00:40:35.000
it can tell us dog,
cat, neither, or both.

00:40:35.000 --> 00:40:37.550
Cat with pancakes--
that is amazing.

00:40:40.080 --> 00:40:41.339
All right.

00:40:41.339 --> 00:40:43.630
And let's see, I have to wait
until it gets to the end.

00:40:43.630 --> 00:40:45.690
I have to know if the
cats or the dogs win.

00:40:48.414 --> 00:40:49.930
Too much anticipation.

00:40:54.182 --> 00:40:56.390
All right, I'm just going
to let it keep on chugging,

00:40:56.390 --> 00:41:00.130
and we'll come back
to this in a second.

00:41:00.130 --> 00:41:01.909
So what did we learn today?

00:41:01.909 --> 00:41:03.950
The big things that I want
you to come away with,

00:41:03.950 --> 00:41:06.199
and I'm hoping that you came
away with, from this talk

00:41:06.199 --> 00:41:09.140
is that we really want you to
be able to focus on your code.

00:41:09.140 --> 00:41:11.117
You know, as
developers, we really

00:41:11.117 --> 00:41:13.200
have to be thinking of our
customers all the time.

00:41:13.200 --> 00:41:15.540
We have to be focusing on
doing what's best for them,

00:41:15.540 --> 00:41:17.970
building our applications,
focusing on our business.

00:41:17.970 --> 00:41:20.334
Shouldn't have to focus
on infrastructure.

00:41:20.334 --> 00:41:22.000
And that's one of the
goals, ultimately,

00:41:22.000 --> 00:41:23.624
that we have with App Engine.

00:41:23.624 --> 00:41:26.040
I want you to be able to focus
not only just on your code,

00:41:26.040 --> 00:41:26.794
but any code.

00:41:26.794 --> 00:41:28.710
It shouldn't be limited
to the seven languages

00:41:28.710 --> 00:41:29.220
that we support.

00:41:29.220 --> 00:41:30.330
We want you to be able
to bring in anything.

00:41:30.330 --> 00:41:31.121
Bring in your Perl.

00:41:31.121 --> 00:41:32.010
Bring your Swift.

00:41:32.010 --> 00:41:33.630
Bring your Elixir,
whatever you need

00:41:33.630 --> 00:41:36.540
to run your application inside
of the cloud to solve problems

00:41:36.540 --> 00:41:37.800
for your customers.

00:41:37.800 --> 00:41:39.675
We want you to be able
to break beyond PaaS.

00:41:39.675 --> 00:41:41.550
So a lot of the limits
that you traditionally

00:41:41.550 --> 00:41:44.190
think of when you run into a
Platform as a Service offering,

00:41:44.190 --> 00:41:45.330
you shouldn't have
to put up with it.

00:41:45.330 --> 00:41:47.413
There should be ways to
use custom amounts of CPU,

00:41:47.413 --> 00:41:49.950
your own languages,
bring your own container.

00:41:49.950 --> 00:41:52.630
And lastly, we want to be
able to liberate intelligence.

00:41:52.630 --> 00:41:55.200
So we have access, now, to
things like the Speech API,

00:41:55.200 --> 00:41:57.907
the Video API, the Vision API.

00:41:57.907 --> 00:42:00.240
You can now use these, fold
them into your applications,

00:42:00.240 --> 00:42:03.070
and use them to help solve real
problems for your customers.

00:42:03.070 --> 00:42:05.070
All the stuff that we did
today, as I mentioned,

00:42:05.070 --> 00:42:05.812
is up on GitHub.

00:42:05.812 --> 00:42:07.770
There's a bunch more
information at these links

00:42:07.770 --> 00:42:08.742
you have over here.

00:42:08.742 --> 00:42:10.450
If you have questions
for us, if you ever

00:42:10.450 --> 00:42:13.440
want talk to the App Engine
team, we're always available.

00:42:13.440 --> 00:42:14.970
We're always on our Slack room.

00:42:14.970 --> 00:42:18.030
You can go here, to
gcp-slack.appspot.com to sign

00:42:18.030 --> 00:42:18.840
up for that.

00:42:18.840 --> 00:42:21.570
And we're happy to answer
questions for you any time.

00:42:21.570 --> 00:42:22.830
And thank you.

